  "worked_solution": "Notice that we are given an equation of the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(x).\n$$\n\nThe right-hand side is a function of $x$ only. This means that, for a fixed value of $x,$ the slope does not vary with $y.$ \n\nNow, let's check each statement:\n\n- Statement I is true. Consider a point of the form $(1,y)$ where $y$ is arbitrary. We have\n\n$$\n{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(1,y)}=1-{1}^{3}=0,\n$$\n\nand this implies that the slope at the point $(1,y)$ is horizontal for any $y.$\n\n- Statement II is true. Consider a point of the form $(0,y)$ where $y$ is arbitrary. We have\n\n$$\n{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(0,y)}=1-{0}^{3}=1>0,\n$$\n\nand this implies that the slope of the slope field at the point $(0,y)$ is positive for any $y.$ Moreover, the slope at the point $(0,y)$ is equal to $1$ for any $y.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Consider a point of the form $(x,2)$ where $x$ is arbitrary. We have\n\n$$\n{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(x,2)}=1-{x}^{3},\n$$\n\nand this implies that the slope of the slope field at the point $(x,2)$ depends on $x.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\"\n\nWe can use the information given by the true statements to begin sketching the slope field, as shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040080/assets/85d8f71c4680a56d5722b87fa5f7b2a7623fd2457f540c2cd15cd3bfa29694f3.png)",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
